{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 49, :comments ["<b>And they encamped by the Jordan, from Beth-ha-Yeshimoth to Abel-ha-Shittim.</b> By way of allusion one may explain that Scripture mentions these two extremities because this pericope speaks of the death of Aaron, and the Sages said (Rosh HaShanah 18a) that the death of the righteous is equivalent to the burning of the house of our Elohim. And this matter contains an allusion at the beginning of the pericope: ״וַיָּמָת שָׁם בִּשְׁנַת הָאַרְבָּעִים וְגו׳ בַּחֹדֶשׁ הַחֲמִישִׁי בְּאֶחָד לַחֹדֶשׁ״ — this is the month of Av, in which the house of our Elohim was burned, and it was fixed as a time of weeping for generations, from the time of the spies, in order to teach that these two are equivalent; therefore they coincided in one month. And this is what is alluded to in ״מִבֵּית הַיְשִׁימוֹת״, for it is an expression of desolation and the destruction of the House. And ״אָבֵל הַשִּׁטִּים״ is an expression of cedar, an allusion to the time in which they mourned over the righteous man who is likened to a cedar, and the two of them chanced upon the same inn. And the joining of these two extremities teaches that they are equivalent — as though one extremity were feeling ״אָבֵל הַשִּׁטִּים״, that is, the mourning over the soul of the righteous, and the other extremity were feeling, with pain, ״בֵּית הַיְשִׁימוֹת״." "And you should know that in the Yalkut on Isaiah (29) it brings under the verse ״הִנְנִי יוֹסִיף לְהַפְלִיא אֶת הָעָם הַזֶּה״ (Isaiah 29:14): 'The departure of the righteous from before Him who spoke and the world came into being is greater than the ninety-eight curses that are in Deuteronomy and than the destruction of the Temple.' This is difficult, one against the other, for the Sages said in tractate Rosh HaShanah that they are equivalent, whereas here it says 'the departure of the righteous is greater.' But look to the right and see: for in the midrash it uses the expression 'their departure' (סִלּוּקָן), whereas in the Gemara they said 'the death of the righteous' (מִיתַת צַדִּיקִים); and in the midrash it uses the expression 'destruction of the Temple' (חֻרְבַּן בֵּית הַמִּקְדָּשׁ), whereas in the Gemara they said 'the burning of the house of our Elohim' (כִּשְׂרֵפַת בֵּית אֱלֹהֵינוּ)." "And the explanation for all of this is as follows. The expression ״חֻרְבַּן בֵּית הַמִּקְדָּשׁ״ ('destruction of the Temple') refers to the time when the material substance of the House — its wood and stones — remains standing, but it is considered destroyed because all its splendor and majesty have departed from it, that is, the departure of all the spiritual things that were in it from the House. But the expression ״שְׂרֵפַת בֵּית אֱלֹהֵינוּ״ ('burning of the house of our Elohim') is harsher than this, for in addition to the departure of the spirituality from there, the material substance was also burned. So too with the righteous: when the expression 'death' is used, it speaks of the material, for the body dies but the soul does not die, for it lives; but the expression 'departure' (סִלּוּק) applies to the soul and not to the body, for it alone ascends, as it is stated (Ecclesiastes 3:21): ״רוּחַ בְּנֵי הָאָדָם הָעוֹלָה הִיא לְמָעְלָה״. Now, the death of the righteous — that is, the thing that touches the material substance of the righteous one's body — this is certainly equivalent to the burning of the house of our Elohim, the material substance of the Temple. But the departure of the righteous — that is, the spirit of the children of man that departs and ascends on high — this is comparable to the destruction of the house of our Elohim, that is, the spirituality from there." "And what it says, that this is greater than that, does not mean that this is greater in essence, but rather that the damage that reaches the world through the departure of the righteous one's soul is greater than the damage that reaches the world through the departure of the spirituality from the Temple. For one must examine precisely what it said — 'before Him who spoke and the world came into being' — why did it not simply say 'before the Holy One, blessed be He'? Rather, it surely comes to give the reason why this is greater than that, and it said: because the Holy One, blessed be He, spoke and the world came into being and desires its existence and its continuance, and the soul of the righteous gives more existence and continuance to the world than all the spirituality that was in the Temple — for one righteous person is a foundation of the world, and his soul is hewn from beneath the Throne of Glory; it possesses great holiness and grants existence and continuance to the entire world. And you may know that this is so, for behold, it brings upon this the verse ״הִנְנִי יוֹסִיף לְהַפְלִיא אֶת הָעָם הַזֶּה הַפְלֵא וָפֶלֶא וְאָבְדָה חָכְמַת חֲכָמָיו״ — and what does this mean, that it speaks of the departure of the righteous? Rather, it surely speaks of the departure of the rational soul, which it called ״חָכְמַת חֲכָמָיו״, for the rational soul grants continuance and existence to the entire world, and its departure causes the pillars of the world to totter. And this is a true and correct matter, worthy of joining to this verse by way of allusion."], :book :numbers, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 33, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:44:09.515831314Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}